{"kind":"artifact","text":"The application-neutral host vocabulary and repository state, at exact head 2867e08818c68e9144bac947bb70f122f075c927 on request/chess-item3-public-api. It holds what a binding record is, who may record one, which one is in force, and the configuration a checkout needs to reopen its own log, and it imports no application profile, so a program that has never heard of Workroom can read a binding a Workroom build wrote. Two of the three defects the previous review found are closed here. BindingInForce now takes the revision whose first-parent history it scans instead of always consulting the ref: a caller that has verified a frontier names that commit, so the binding it selects comes out of the history it verified, and a caller with no verified frontier — internal/app, whose audit runs later when the fold first reads the log — names the ref and gets exactly what it got before. An empty revision is refused rather than guessed. And the genesis payload ceiling no longer narrows into a negative read limit: readLimit clamps a ceiling above MaxInt64 to MaxInt64, which loses nothing because no blob Git can store reaches that size. Before the clamp, a repository initialized with such a ceiling passed over every binding record as unreadable and then opened as bound to nothing at all. Conditions met: go test -count=1 ./... clean at this exact head, go vet ./... clean; the regression in internal/app proves the answer follows the revision named — earlier frontier, ref, and empty — and the host regression proves a ceiling above the signed limit still opens. Both were mutation-checked: reverting either fix fails its test.","body":{"branch":"request/chess-item3-public-api","commit":"2867e08818c68e9144bac947bb70f122f075c927","path":"internal/apphost"}}